-- Mega Siddur deep dive: verses before Birkat HaTorah. Rows 47-50 in siddur_commentary
-- (inserted 2026-10-22; deep dive expanded the same day with the Rishonim, the Tur's reform and printed siddurim from 1599-1900).

update public.siddur_commentary set
  title = 'Verses before the blessing: which siddurim get the order right, from Rav Amram to Koren',
  source = 'Seder Rav Amram (Hedegard); Machzor Vitry; Rambam Tefillah 7; Tur & Beit Yosef OC 46; SA 46:9 with Rema, Magen Avraham, Gra; Maaseh Rav; MB 46:27–28; Arukh HaShulchan 46; Kaf HaChayim 46; siddur scans 1599–1900; Rinat Yisrael; Koren; Chabad; Sefaria siddurim',
  body = $body$Rabbi Elyada made the point that only the Koren siddur gets this right: it doesn't put verses like רֵאשִׁית חָכְמָה יִרְאַת ה׳ (Tehillim 111:10) before Birkat HaTorah. Many siddurim print that verse right after Modeh Ani, so the first verse of Tanakh you say each morning comes before the blessing over Torah. To test the claim, this traces the question from the Geonim to today's siddurim, using the books themselves where possible: scans on archive.org, the Rinat Yisrael PDF on Daat, and the siddur texts on Sefaria.

**The short answer**

- Koren isn't alone. **Rinat Yisrael** (Ashkenaz) has the same order, and so do **Chabad's Tehillat Hashem** and **Edot HaMizrach**.
- But Koren's order is younger than it looks. It is the Tur's personal reform from the 14th century, adopted by the Rema. The older order, which the Gra defended, put Birkat HaTorah *after* all the morning blessings.
- Verses before Birkat HaTorah are also very old: Machzor Vitry (around 1100) opens with them. What changed is the Mishnah Berurah: "nowadays everyone is strict."

**How the order developed**

- **Seder Rav Amram Gaon (9th century)**, in [Hedegard's critical edition from the manuscripts](https://archive.org/details/DavidHedegardSederR.AmramGaonLindstedt1951): washing, then the morning blessings through *hagomel chasadim*, then Birkat HaTorah. No verses are said first. Amos 4:12 is quoted only as a proof text for washing.
- **[Machzor Vitry](https://www.sefaria.org/Machzor_Vitry%2C_Order_of_Shacharit)** (school of Rashi, around 1100) opens with ואני ברב חסדך, מה טבו, שמחתי באומרים לי, האזינה and בקר תשמע קולי. All of these are verses, said *before* washing, the blessings and Birkat HaTorah. This is the Ma Tovu custom about 900 years ago.
- **Rambam** ([Tefillah 7:10–11](https://www.sefaria.org/Mishneh_Torah%2C_Prayer_and_the_Priestly_Blessing.7.10)): Birkat HaTorah comes after the morning blessings. In a responsum (Pe'er HaDor 104, cited by Kaf HaChayim 46:52) he rules: "one must always bless over the Torah before reading, *even one verse*, and there is no difference if he reads it as supplication."
- **[Tur OC 46](https://www.sefaria.org/Tur%2C_Orach_Chayim.46)** describes the order of his day. The morning blessings were followed by לעולם יהא אדם, שמע ישראל and אתה הוא, and "afterwards Birkat HaTorah and the Tamid." Then he writes: "*it was difficult for me, this order of saying verses before Birkat HaTorah, so I made it my own practice to say Birkat HaTorah right after Elokai Neshama.*" This is where the Koren order comes from.
- **[Beit Yosef](https://www.sefaria.org/Beit_Yosef%2C_Orach_Chayim.46)** lays out the dispute. Maharam (via the Agur): verses said as supplication need no blessing. Raavad: verses are Mikra and need the blessing. Maharil: first said they need it. In a later responsum he wrote there is no concern, and that this is the custom in Ashkenaz. Orchot Chaim: in the Ten Days, make the blessing before Selichot.
- **[Shulchan Aruch 46:9](https://www.sefaria.org/Shulchan_Arukh%2C_Orach_Chayim.46.9):** the Mechaber leans strict ("it is proper to be concerned for the first view"). The Rema says the custom is lenient (Selichot, and the verses on entering shul), but "the custom is to put Birkat HaTorah right after Asher Yatzar, and one should not change it." That is the Tur's order.
- **The Gra** ([Biur HaGra 46:9](https://www.sefaria.org/Beur_HaGra_on_Shulchan_Arukh%2C_Orach_Chayim.46.9)) pushes back. "*In all the books, early and late*" (the Rambam, Abudarham, the Rosh, Rabbeinu Tam's Sefer HaYashar), Birkat HaTorah comes after all the blessings, before the Tamid. The Tur's objection only holds on the strict view. [Maaseh Rav §8](https://www.sefaria.org/Maaseh_Rav) records his practice: Birkat HaTorah just before Parashat HaTamid.
- **Magen Avraham 46**, quoting the Levush: communities that say Birkat HaTorah right after Asher Yatzar evidently hold that verses are forbidden before it, so they should say it even before Selichot.
- **[Mishnah Berurah 46:27–28](https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Berurah.46.28):** "nowadays the world is strict like the Tur, not to say *any* verses before Birkat HaTorah."
- **[Arukh HaShulchan 46](https://www.sefaria.org/Arukh_HaShulchan%2C_Orach_Chaim.46):** the printed siddurim put Ma Tovu, Adon Olam and Yigdal before everything. "*Although it isn't printed that way in the siddurim, and most people say it as printed, the main thing is as I wrote*": Birkat HaTorah after Elokai Neshama, or after all the morning blessings.
- **[Kaf HaChayim 46:52–53, 59](https://www.sefaria.org/Kaf_HaChayim_on_Shulchan_Arukh%2C_Orach_Chayim.46)** (Sephardi): The Mechaber's strict ruling is the halacha. Those who say ויהי נעם before every mitzvah should not say it before Birkat HaTorah (Birkei Yosef). Say ותתפלל חנה only after it (Yosef Ometz). Don't say ואני ברוב חסדך at the shul door before it. Following the Arizal, Birkat HaTorah comes at the end of the morning blessings.

**What the siddurim print**

- **Seder HaYom** (R. Moshe ibn Makhir, Venice 1599), where Modeh Ani first appears ([1876 reprint](https://archive.org/details/sefersederhayoms008800/page/n7)): "ובקומו יאמר מיד מודה אני…" and then straight to the kavanot for washing. No ראשית חכמה.
- **Heidenheim, Safa Berura** ([Rödelheim 1806](https://archive.org/details/siddur-safah-berurah-wolf-heidenheim-rodelheim-1806-images/page/n14)): Modeh Ani (citing the Yaavetz), washing, tzitzit, tallit, tefillin, then מה טבו, Adon Olam and Yigdal. After that come the washing blessing, Asher Yatzar, Birkat HaTorah, then Elokai Neshama. A rubric before Birkat HaTorah reads "in many Ashkenaz communities they begin right away with Elokai Neshama." So: no ראשית חכמה, but many verses before the blessing. ✘
- **Baer, Avodat Yisrael** ([Rödelheim 1868](https://archive.org/details/Seder_Avodat_Yisrael_1868/page/n58)): no Modeh Ani at all. It opens at the shul door with ואני ברב חסדך, בבית אלהים and מה טבו, then more verses "before Shacharit," then Shachar Avakeshcha, Adon Olam and Yigdal (pp. 33–35). Asher Yatzar and Birkat HaTorah follow (pp. 36–37), with the same rubric. ✘
- **Siddur HaGra** ([Jerusalem 1900](https://archive.org/details/siddur-ha-gra-jerusalem-1900-images/page/n40)): Ma Tovu and Adon Olam, the morning blessings, לעולם יהא אדם, שמע ישראל and אתה הוא, then Birkat HaTorah before the Tamid. Its gloss says so: "*according to his view that Birkat HaTorah is said before Parashat HaTamid.*" This is the old order, on purpose. ✘ by the Mishnah Berurah's standard, ✔ by the Gra's.
- **ArtScroll (Complete, Ashkenaz)**, per a published page guide: tzitzit and tallit (p. 2), tefillin (p. 4), Ma Tovu, Adon Olam and Yigdal (p. 12), then Birchos HaTorah (p. 14). This is the Heidenheim/Baer layout. ✘
- **Olat Re'iyah** (Rav Kook), going by the order of its commentary on Sefaria: Modeh Ani, tzitzit, tefillin, Ma Tovu, Adon Olam and Yigdal, then washing, Asher Yatzar and Birkat HaTorah. No ראשית חכמה. ✘ (Ma Tovu)
- **Rinat Yisrael** (Ashkenaz, Moreshet; [PDF on Daat](https://www.daat.ac.il/daat/sidurim/rinat-israel01.pdf), pp. 17–24): Modeh Ani, washing, Asher Yatzar, Elokai Neshama, Birkat HaTorah with Birkat Kohanim and אלו דברים. Tzitzit, tallit, tefillin (with קדש and והיה כי יביאך) and Ma Tovu come after. The only verse beforehand, שויתי ה׳ לנגדי תמיד, is quoted in the instructions as a motto, not printed to be said. ✔
- **Koren Shalem (Ashkenaz):** Modeh Ani, washing, Asher Yatzar, Elokai Neshama, tzitzit, Birkat HaTorah. Tallit, tefillin and Ma Tovu come after. ✔
- **Chabad, Tehillat Hashem:** Modeh Ani, washing, Asher Yatzar, Elokai Neshama, the morning blessings, Birkat HaTorah, with the warning "it is forbidden to utter words of Torah until one has blessed." ✔
- **Edot HaMizrach** (Sefaria's edition, following the Ben Ish Chai): Modeh Ani, the morning blessings, Birkat HaTorah. Tallit, tefillin and korbanot come later. The Moroccan nusach on Wikisource (from the Chida) puts the blessings straight through to ואני אברכם, following the Arizal. ✔
- **Siddur Ashkenaz on Sefaria** (the text this Mega Siddur shows): ראשית חכמה and ברוך שם directly after Modeh Ani. Otherwise Birkat HaTorah comes early. ✘ (one verse)
- **Siddur Sefard on Sefaria**, and the linear Weekday Siddur Sefard: ראשית חכמה "after washing his hands," then tallit (ברכי נפשי, מה יקר), tefillin (וארשתיך) and then the full parshiyot of קדש and והיה כי יביאך. Only after Ma Tovu, Adon Olam and Yigdal do the morning blessings and Birkat HaTorah come. ✘ This one is the furthest from the Mishnah Berurah: reading Chumash before the blessing. (The custom of reading those two parshiyot in tefillin goes back to a chasid cited by the Bach, OC 38, via Eliyah Rabbah 46.)

**Where did ראשית חכמה come from?**

It isn't in Seder HaYom, which introduced Modeh Ani. It isn't in Heidenheim, Baer, the Siddur HaGra, Rinat Yisrael, Koren or Chabad either. It does appear in the Siddur Ashkenaz and Siddur Sefard on Sefaria, which instruct it "after washing." This deep dive hasn't yet traced its first printing. The likeliest candidates are the 18th–19th-century Chasidic and kabbalistic siddurim.

**An aside:** the Maharshal (Responsa 64, via Eliyah Rabbah 46) skipped מה טבו entirely, because Bilaam said it, and he said it as a curse.

**In practice:** say Birkat HaTorah right after Asher Yatzar and Elokai Neshama, as the Tur did, the Rema records as the custom, and the Mishnah Berurah calls today's practice. Then ראשית חכמה, Ma Tovu, the tallit verses and קדש all come after it, whatever your siddur prints.

**Still to check:** Ishei Yisrael (the modern Gra siddur), Kol Eliyahu, Ish Matzliach, the Yaavetz's own siddur, early Sephardi prints (Bomberg 1519/1524), and the Romaniote and Italian manuscripts on archive.org. hebrewbooks.org rate-limited this search partway through. Corrections welcome.$body$
where id = 47;

update public.siddur_commentary set body = $body$This is Tehillim 111:10, said here before the Torah blessings. The Shulchan Aruch (OC 46:9) says not to say verses before Birkat HaTorah even as prayer. The Mishnah Berurah (46:28) says that today everyone is strict about this.

The line isn't in Seder HaYom, where Modeh Ani first appears (Venice 1599). It also isn't in Heidenheim (1806), Baer (1868), the Siddur HaGra (1900), Rinat Yisrael, Koren or Chabad's Tehillat Hashem.

See the deep dive on Torah Blessings for how each siddur, from Rav Amram to Koren, orders it.$body$ where id = 48;
